                     Release Notes for SAMRAI v4.6.0

     (notes for previous releases may be found in /SAMRAI/docs/release)

*****************************************************************************


Where to report Bugs
--------------------

If a bug is found in the SAMRAI library, we ask that you kindly report
it to us so that we may fix it.

Please post an issue on github https://github.com/LLNL/SAMRAI

*****************************************************************************



****************************************************************************

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
                Significant bug fixes
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) RefineSchedule no longer applies overly restrictive conditions when
computing overlaps between source and destination patches that touch at
their boundaries but do not overlap in their interiors.  These overlaps
are required for data types centered on cell boundaries, including node,
face, side, and edge data.  This fix may change numerical results; the
changes should improve accuracy by adding overlaps that were previously
missed.  The GitHub pull request for this fix is
https://github.com/llnl/SAMRAI/pull/312

2) A bug where using the artificial_minimum_load parameter did not work
when the use_vouchers option set to TRUE in CascadePartitioner has been fixed.
artificial_minimum_load now works consistently with both the default
BoxTransitSet path and the optional use_vouchers path.

3) IntVector::sortIntVector previously did not work correctly for multiblock
IntVectors.  It sorted every block's values according to the sorting order
computed for Block 0.  This has been fixed to sort each block's values
independently.  The method also relied on an incorrect assumption that
the calling `this` IntVector had the same number of blocks as the input
IntVector.  This caused the risk of size-based memory access errors.  Since
the method's contract is fully state-replacing for the calling object, it
has been fixed to check the size of the calling IntVector and resize it 
to the size of the input IntVector when necessary.

4) A bug in ConduitDatabase::getBoolVector which incorrectly queried Conduit
to obtain the number of elements in the input Conduit node has been fixed.



*****************************************************************************


****************************************************************************

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
                Summary of what's new
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) BoxContainer now provides move construction, move assignment, and
emplaceFront/emplaceBack operations for constructing Boxes in place.
Box also has a move constructor.

2) CellIndex, NodeIndex, EdgeIndex, FaceIndex, and SideIndex now provide
type-safe arithmetic operators within each derived type.  Invalid
arithmetic between different derived index types is rejected.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
                Summary of what's changed
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) A broad set of low-level classes in hier and tbox has been modernized
with constexpr and noexcept functions, explicitly defaulted or deleted
special member functions, standard-library array/optional storage, and
move-aware implementations.

2) Implicit conversion from Index to IntVector has been disabled by making
the IntVector constructor taking an Index explicit.  This is an API change
that may affect call sites in user codes.

3) The minimum C++ standard for the source and test code is now C++20.

4) BlueprintUtils and PatchHierarchy now provide expanded support for
Conduit Blueprint meshes, including application-supplied field data,
configurable topology names, and detailed coarse/fine adjacency extents.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
                Details about what's new
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) BoxContainer emplace operations forward Box constructor arguments and
invalidate its cached search tree when needed.  Move operations transfer
the container storage and state without copying all Boxes.  These additions
are intended to reduce temporary Box construction in hierarchy and mesh
algorithms.

2) Arithmetic overloads for the data-centered index classes check dimension
and, where applicable, axis compatibility.  The overloads prevent accidental
operations, such as adding a NodeIndex to a CellIndex, while retaining
operations using a single derived type, as well as operations that use
hier::Index and a derived type.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
                Details about what's changed
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

1) The modernization of hier and tbox replaces a number of hand-written
constructors, destructors, and copy operations with compiler-generated
special members.  Several internal raw-pointer arrays and manually managed
objects now use std::array, std::optional, std::unique_ptr, or move
operations.  This includes Box, Index, IntVector, DatabaseBox,
BoxContainer, and BoxLevel-related objects.

2) Implicit conversion from Index to IntVector has been disabled by making
the IntVector constructor taking an Index explicit.  Any user codes relying
on this implicit conversion will need to change to explicitly construct an
IntVector.  This change protects against potentially bug-prone confusion
of these two similar types, as well as helping to avoid repeated
silent invocations of the IntVector constructor caused by usage such as
passing an Index for a function argument that is declared IntVector.

3) The minimum C++ standard for the source and test code is now C++20.  In
particular, variable-length arrays have been replaced with standard-library
containers, and deprecated random_shuffle use in the tree search performance
test has been replaced with std::shuffle.

4) BlueprintUtils::putFieldsToDatabase walks the full hierarchy and limits
field output to the visible portions represented by the flattened hierarchy.
For each appropriate Blueprint domain, it calls the application-provided
BlueprintUtilsStrategy::putFieldsToDomainDatabase callback.  The strategy
supplies a default no-op implementation, so existing mesh-only Blueprint
output remains supported.  BlueprintUtils also stores a configurable
topology name and provides access to it through setTopologyName() and
getTopologyName(), replacing use of hard-coded strings for this name.

PatchHierarchy::makeAdjacencySets() now computes partial coarse/fine overlap
extents independently in every active coordinate direction.  The previous
scalar partial_lo and partial_hi values are replaced by dimension-sized
IntVectors; a positive-modulo calculation handles negative coordinates and
each direction is adjusted independently.  The resulting values are stored
as per-direction Blueprint databases on the fine-side window.  This
preserves alignment information when a corner of a fine domain does not
align with a node of a coarse domain at a coarse-fine interface.



